[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate · 1:30] Sure. Since revenue is growing but margin is collapsing, the issue is almost certainly on the cost side, but I want to confirm revenue quality first.

I'd structure it three ways. First, revenue: is the 15% growth coming from new stores, more transactions per store, or price increases? Growth from new store openings could mask weak same-store performance and bring heavy upfront costs.

Second, costs: split into COGS — coffee beans, milk, cups — and operating costs like labor, rent, utilities, marketing, and corporate overhead. I'd look at each as a percentage of revenue two years ago versus today to see which lines expanded.

Third, mix: has the product mix shifted toward lower-margin items, or has channel mix shifted, say toward delivery apps that take a commission?

To start, can I get the cost structure as a percent of revenue for both years, and whether the 200 stores include recent openings?
