[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:33] Okay. You asked about that — 200 stores today — and it was 200 stores two years ago, so the footprint hasn’t changed. Hold that request; I'll come back to it shortly. Before I give you data, is that structure MECE — what, if anything, is missing or overlapping?

[candidate · 3:03] Fair challenge. A couple of gaps.

My buckets overlap a bit — channel mix drives both revenue and cost, since delivery commissions could sit in either. I'd pick one home for it and be consistent, probably as a cost line.

What's missing: below-the-line items. Net margin, not operating margin, so interest expense, depreciation, one-time charges, and taxes all sit between operating profit and the 6%. If they took on debt or wrote something off, that's a cause my tree wouldn't catch. I should add a fourth branch for non-operating items.

Also, with a flat footprint, I'd want same-store revenue versus any closures and reopenings inside that 200, and whether some stores are dragging while others are fine — so a distribution view, not just averages.

So: revenue drivers, operating costs, mix, and non-operating items. That's cleaner.
