[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 8:10] Menu prices have not changed in two years. The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit. You asked about that — Total revenue is $480M a year.

[candidate · 9:40] That confirms it. Revenue up 15% with ticket up 10% means transactions only grew about 4.5% — so growth is mostly basket size, not traffic. And none of it is price.

Let me size the fix. Take revenue as 100: COGS 58, labor 22, overhead 14, profit 6. If I raise prices and hold volume, COGS dollars stay flat, and labor and overhead are largely fixed too. So profit = R − 94.

To get back to 24%: R − 94 = 0.24R, so 0.76R = 94, R = 123.7. That's a 24% price increase — unrealistic without losing customers.

A 10% increase gets revenue to 110, profit to 16, margin 14.5%. That feels more achievable, especially since customers are already spending more per visit, which suggests some willingness to pay.

Do we have any elasticity data, or competitor pricing?
