[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 9:42] I don't have elasticity data or competitor pricing. Let's keep to what we have. You said a 10% increase feels achievable given customers are already spending more per visit. Walk me through why higher basket size supports willingness to pay on price.

[candidate · 11:12] Honestly, it's suggestive rather than conclusive, and I should flag that.

The argument: ticket is up 10% purely on items per visit, not price. Customers are choosing to add a pastry or size up. That's discretionary spend — they're not trading down or trimming the basket, which is what I'd expect if this base were price-stressed. Traffic also held and grew slightly, so demand looks reasonably sturdy.

The weakness: items-per-visit tells me about incremental add-on spend, not about sensitivity to the base price of a drink. Someone might happily add a $2 scone and still walk if their latte goes from $5 to $5.50, because the drink is the habitual purchase and the anchor price. Different decisions.

So I'd treat it as a reason to believe a price increase is survivable, not as evidence of the right magnitude. I'd want to test it — stage increases across a subset of the 200 stores and read traffic before rolling out.
