[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:15] Understood. Beyond raising prices, what else could Brew & Bean do to close the margin gap and protect itself going forward?

[candidate · 12:45] A few levers.

On COGS directly: renegotiate supplier contracts and lock in forward pricing or hedges on coffee so they're not fully exposed next cycle. Two years of unhedged inflation is itself a finding. Also consolidate vendors across 200 stores for scale, and audit waste — spoilage, remakes, portioning — since a point or two of COGS there is real money at $480M.

On mix: steer the basket toward higher-margin items. If dairy and food inflated 37.5%, some items are now barely profitable. I'd want item-level margins to see whether anything should be repriced hard or dropped.

On the non-price revenue side: they've shown they can grow items per visit, so lean into that with attachment and larger sizes, and consider loyalty to protect traffic through a price increase.

On overhead: two points of drift is worth a look, though it's secondary.

Structurally, the real issue is they have no mechanism for passing input costs through. I'd put a regular pricing review in place.
